CAT = "iespElectric"; SEG = "ELE"
# Unit 5 of Safe Pass: risk assessment for electricity. General Application Regulations 2007 Part 3
# (Reg 81: RCD of 30 mA on portable equipment circuits over 125 V); HSA and ESB Networks guidance on
# overhead lines and underground cables.
Q = [
('What protection must a circuit supplying portable tools at more than 125 volts have?',
 'An RCD tripping at 30 mA or less', ['A fuse rated at 30 amps or more', 'A plug fitted with no earth pin', 'A longer extension lead to the board'],
 'Regulation 81 of the General Application Regulations 2007 requires residual current devices with a tripping current not over 30 milliamperes on such circuits, including those fed by generators.'),
("A drill's cable is nicked and current starts flowing through the user to earth. What device should cut the supply?",
 'A residual current device (RCD)', ['A fuse in the plug top', "A transformer's cooling fan", "An extension reel's handle"],
 'An RCD compares the current in the live and neutral conductors and disconnects quickly when some leaks away, for example through a person. A fuse protects the cable, not people.'),
('Why are reduced voltage 110 V tools widely used on construction sites?',
 'A shock from them is far less dangerous', ['They are always battery powered', 'They never need any inspection', 'They can be used without any plug'],
 'The General Application Regulations require a reduced low voltage supply, not over 125 V AC, for portable tools on construction sites. With the transformer midpoint earthed, a 110 V supply puts only about 55 V to earth, which the HSA says reduces the potential level of shock.'),
('A worker notices a cut in the outer sheath of an extension lead. What should they do?',
 'Take it out of use and report it', ['Wrap the cut in insulating tape', 'Use it only in dry weather from now on', 'Swap its plug onto another lead'],
 'Damaged leads must not be used. Report the defect so the lead is repaired or replaced by a competent person; tape is not a repair.'),
('What should be checked before each use of a portable electrical tool?',
 'Cable, plug and casing for damage', ["Its colour and the maker's brand", 'The serial number and nothing else', 'That it was bought in Ireland'],
 'A quick user check finds most faults: cuts or kinks in the cable, a cracked plug or casing, loose parts, and signs of burning or overheating.'),
("A worker's grinder keeps cutting out. A colleague offers to open it up and fix it. What should happen?",
 'A competent electrician should repair it', ['The colleague should fix it at lunch', 'The worker should tape the switch on', 'It should be used until it stops'],
 'Electrical repairs must be done by a competent person. A poor repair can leave a tool live on the casing or with no earth.'),
('A long extension lead must cross a site haul road. How should it be protected?',
 'Raised overhead or in a proper cable protector', ['Laid flat on the road surface', 'Covered with a scaffold board', 'Buried under loose stones'],
 'Leads crossing traffic routes must be protected from being crushed by vehicles, by running them overhead or through a purpose-made cable ramp.'),
('Why is water a particular danger with electrical equipment on site?',
 'It makes electric shock more likely', ['It makes power tools much heavier', 'It switches off RCDs for good', 'It lowers the voltage of the supply'],
 "Wet skin and wet surfaces lower the body's resistance, so more current flows in a shock. Keep equipment and connections dry and protected."),
('A small excavator is about to dig a trench beside a footpath. What should be done before digging starts?',
 'Get the service drawings and scan the line', ['Dig a test hole by machine first', 'Look for warning tape after the dig', 'Ask the owner of the next house'],
 'HSA and ESB Networks guidance: get the service drawings, scan with a cable locator before and during the dig, and hand dig near any cable shown.'),
('Near a known buried cable, how should the final digging be done?',
 'Carefully by hand', ['With the excavator bucket', 'With a pickaxe', 'With a pneumatic breaker'],
 "Once a cable's position is known, expose it by careful hand digging. Mechanical excavators and picks can cut straight through cables."),
('A worker digging finds a cable that is not on the drawings. What should they do?',
 'Stop and treat it as live', ['Cut it as it is probably dead', 'Move it aside by hand', 'Cover it and keep going'],
 'Treat every cable as live until a competent person proves otherwise. Stop work in that area and report it.'),
('Why are overhead power lines a major hazard on sites?',
 'Electricity can arc across a gap', ['They block mobile phone signals', 'They attract lightning and nothing else', 'They always carry a low voltage'],
 'High-voltage lines can arc to tipper bodies, booms, scaffold tubes and ladders that come close without touching. Many deaths involve plant under lines.'),
('What should be in place where plant must pass under overhead lines?',
 'Goal posts and barriers at a safe height', ['A sign telling drivers to go slowly underneath', 'A spotter standing on the cab roof', 'Nothing, if the driver is careful'],
 'The Code of Practice for Avoiding Danger from Overhead Electricity Lines requires crossing points with height-restricting goalposts of rigid, non-conducting material on each side of the line, in line with the barriers that keep plant away.'),
('A tipper truck touches an overhead line. What should the driver do if it is safe to stay?',
 'Stay in the cab until the power is off', ['Climb down slowly holding the door', 'Step out and run to get help', 'Touch the ground with a shovel first, then step out'],
 'The overhead lines code of practice says stop, stay in the cab, keep others away and call ESB Networks on 1800 372 999. Only if fire or another hazard forces them out should they jump clear, feet together, without touching vehicle and ground at once.'),
('Who should be contacted for advice before working near ESB overhead lines?',
 'ESB Networks', ["The HSA's helpline only", 'The local fire brigade', 'The Garda station'],
 'The network owner, ESB Networks, must be consulted before work near its lines. The Code of Practice for Avoiding Danger from Overhead Electricity Lines, published by ESB Networks and approved by the HSA, sets out exclusion zones and precautions.'),
('Besides burns and the shock itself, what other injury does the HSA warn an electric shock can cause?',
 'A fall, such as from a ladder', ['Hearing loss from the noise', 'Hand-arm vibration syndrome', 'Dermatitis from the cable'],
 'The HSA guide to the electricity regulations lists secondary injuries from muscle spasms during a shock, for example falling from a ladder after a mild shock, among the electrical hazards.'),
('A labourer is stuck to a damaged live lead and cannot let go. What should a co-worker do first?',
 'Switch off the supply', ['Pull them away by the arm', 'Throw water over them', 'Start CPR straight away'],
 'Touching them would put you in the circuit too. Isolate the supply, or if that is impossible move them with dry non-conductive material, then call 112 or 999, the emergency numbers the Gardaí give.'),
('What should be done before work starts on an electrical installation?',
 'Isolate it and lock it off', ['Turn it down to half power', 'Tell the client by email', 'Wear gloves and work live'],
 'HSA guidance on Regulation 85 says an isolator-locking device must be used so the equipment cannot become live again, and isolated conductors should be proved dead with a suitable tester at the work location before work starts.'),
('Why should a worker not overload a socket with adaptors?',
 'It can overheat and start a fire', ['It lowers the voltage to the tools', "It trips the scaffold's alarm", 'It wears out the tool motor faster'],
 'Too many appliances on one outlet draw more current than it is rated for, heating the socket and cables.'),
('What does an electrical warning sign on site look like?',
 'Yellow triangle, black lightning flash', ['Blue circle with a white flash', 'Green square with a white flash', 'Red circle with a black flash'],
 'Warning signs are yellow triangles with black symbols. Blue circles show something you must do and red circles show something you must not do.'),
]
